WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.730 --> 00:00:04.890
Last month, your agent spent an hour on a broken deploy.

2
00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:07.980
Today it hits the same wall, and starts from zero.

3
00:00:08.860 --> 00:00:11.390
Agents don't forget because they're careless.

4
00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:15.390
They forget because nothing puts yesterday's lesson in front of them.

5
00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:17.980
Notes get written. Nobody reads them.

6
00:00:18.860 --> 00:00:21.490
Claude Note closes that loop.

7
00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:29.820
It captures what your agents learn, keeps it as plain Markdown notes, and now, before the agent acts, it brings the right ones back.

8
00:00:30.710 --> 00:00:33.210
This is a real Claude Code session.

9
00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:37.770
We ask if it's fine to run the production build on our four-gig server.

10
00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:46.780
Before the model even sees the prompt, a hook searches the notes, in about a second, and finds one: we decided never to build on that box.

11
00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:49.750
The agent opens the note, then answers.

12
00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:54.100
No. Build on the laptop, ship the artifact.

13
00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:57.720
Same model, without recall?

14
00:00:57.770 --> 00:01:00.440
You can, but. The rule was ours.

15
00:01:00.490 --> 00:01:02.890
Only recall could bring it.

16
00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:06.000
Codex gets the same hook.

17
00:01:06.050 --> 00:01:09.050
Same question, same answer, with a link to the note.

18
00:01:09.950 --> 00:01:13.010
Finding the right note is the hard part.

19
00:01:13.060 --> 00:01:17.780
Keyword search is fast, but one unusual word returns nothing.

20
00:01:17.830 --> 00:01:25.700
So recall keeps only the words that matter, tries narrower and narrower queries, and pools everything they find.

21
00:01:25.750 --> 00:01:28.520
And when it finds nothing, it says so.

22
00:01:28.570 --> 00:01:30.650
Silence reads like nothing is known.

23
00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:34.840
When a command fails, the sentinel steps in.

24
00:01:34.890 --> 00:01:46.200
It reads the error, finds the note that fixed it last time, and flags the failures that never fix themselves: expired logins, quotas, a full disk.

25
00:01:46.250 --> 00:01:51.290
It reads the output, not the command, so a grep for an error word stays quiet.

26
00:01:52.130 --> 00:01:55.660
Learn something? Write it down while it's fresh.

27
00:01:55.710 --> 00:02:00.300
Remember saves a typed note and indexes it in half a second.

28
00:02:00.350 --> 00:02:03.130
The very next prompt can recall it.

29
00:02:04.030 --> 00:02:06.120
Then prove it works.

30
00:02:06.170 --> 00:02:13.040
Doctor plants a canary note, runs the real hooks against it, and checks the index is fresh.

31
00:02:13.090 --> 00:02:19.240
The first time we ran it on our own machine, it caught our index refresh failing for eight hours.

32
00:02:19.290 --> 00:02:21.850
Everything we wrote that afternoon was invisible.

33
00:02:22.710 --> 00:02:25.080
One command sets it all up.

34
00:02:25.130 --> 00:02:27.640
Your agents already solved this.

35
00:02:27.690 --> 00:02:29.610
With Claude Note, they remember.
